{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.SeparatedGauss","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mathlib.SeparatedGauss","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.MvPolynomial.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.MvPolynomial.Eval","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.MvPolynomial.CommRing","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.GCDMonoid.Finset","lean:module:Mathlib.RingTheory.Localization.Integer","lean:module:Mathlib.RingTheory.Localization.FractionRing","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Rat.Defs","lean:module:Mathlib.RingTheory.Int.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.FieldSimp","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Rat.Lemmas","lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.SeparatedHeight","lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.SeparatedWronskian"],"declarations":16,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.abs_coeff_integral_wronskian_factor_le_right","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.abs_coeff_integral_wronskian_factor_le_right","kind":"theorem","line":404,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SeparatedGauss.lean#L404-L412","doc":"§7's height step for the **right** factor.  Mirror of the previous theorem; the\nnon-vanishing hypothesis swaps sides, because the argument picks a non-zero coefficient of the\n*other* factor. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.abs_coeff_integral_wronskian_factor_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.abs_coeff_integral_wronskian_factor_le","kind":"theorem","line":391,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SeparatedGauss.lean#L391-L401","doc":"**§7's height step, with integrality supplied.**\n\n`H(U') ≤ H(W)` and `H(V') ≤ H(W)` for the integral factors above.  This is the inequality the\ninduction hypothesis needs on the `x`-factor, and the reason §6 does Gauss at all: over `ℚ`\nthe argument — \"a non-zero coefficient has absolute value at least 1\" — is false. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.exists_integral_wronskian_factors_div","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.exists_integral_wronskian_factors_div","kind":"theorem","line":328,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SeparatedGauss.lean#L328-L389","doc":"**The same factorisation, run on the *divided* Wronskian.**\n\n⚠⚠ **The twenty-sixth correction is that this theorem has to exist.**  Correction 21 replaced\nthe Wronskian's height bound: §12's budget covers `W`, the determinant **divided** by\n`∏_i ∏_h (listIdx …)!`, and not the determinant itself, which is larger by a factor\n`2^{Θ(r² log r)}`.  That correction landed in `WronskianHeight` — `exists_divided_wronskian`\nproduces exactly `det = C c · W` with `|coeff W| ≤ r!·S^r·(2^{Σd}·B)^r` — but **it was never\npropagated to the consumers.**  `exists_integral_wronskian_factors` still factors `det`, and so\nthe induction-hypothesis halves built on it take a bound on `det`.  Feeding them the only bound\n§11's clause admits is therefore impossible: the two lemmas do not compose, and the arc had\nboth of them green.\n\nNothing here is deep.  `exists_integral_separated_factorization` was already stated for an\narbitrary integral `W` whose map is a separated product, and\n\n    map W = C c⁻¹ · (renameₗ Wx · renameᵣ Wy) = renameₗ Wx · renameᵣ (C c⁻¹ · Wy)\n\nis that shape, with the constant parked on the `y` side so the `x` half keeps its exact\n`C l · Wx`.  The `y` half's constant is `l⁻¹·c⁻¹`, non-zero, and `index_C_mul` does not care\nwhich constant it is.\n\n⚠ What did not catch this: `lake build` (both lemmas are true), `#print axioms` (both are\nclean), and every witness in the arc (none composes them).  What caught it was trying to write\nthe assembly and finding that `hB`'s `b` had to be two different numbers. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.exists_integral_wronskian_factors","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.exists_integral_wronskian_factors","kind":"theorem","line":307,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SeparatedGauss.lean#L307-L326","doc":"**§6, assembled.**  The separated Wronskian of a representation of an *integer* polynomial\nhas integral factors whose product is the determinant taken over `ℤ`.\n\nThe determinant on the right is formed from `P` itself, over `ℤ`; `map_det_pderivList` is what\nidentifies it with the `ℚ`-valued one the representation produces. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.integral_factor_ne_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.integral_factor_ne_zero","kind":"theorem","line":295,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SeparatedGauss.lean#L295-L300","doc":"Non-vanishing transports to the integral factor. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.degreeOf_integral_factor_le","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.degreeOf_integral_factor_le","kind":"theorem","line":285,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SeparatedGauss.lean#L285-L293","doc":"Degrees transport to the integral factor.  `≤` suffices, which is why the scaling costs\nnothing here: `degreeOf_C_mul_le` is the only step. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.degreeOf_map_of_injective","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.degreeOf_map_of_injective","kind":"theorem","line":278,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SeparatedGauss.lean#L278-L283","doc":"A change of coefficient ring along an injective map does not move degrees. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.exists_integral_separated_factorization","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.exists_integral_separated_factorization","kind":"theorem","line":245,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SeparatedGauss.lean#L245-L270","doc":"**The factorisation is exact over `ℤ`.**\n\n`exists_integral_separated_factors` produces integral factors; this says their product is `W`\non the nose, not merely up to a rational.  That is Mahler's `W = U·V`, and it is what\n`abs_coeff_le_of_mul_separated` consumes — the height step needs the product to *be* `W`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.exists_integral_separated_factors","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.exists_integral_separated_factors","kind":"theorem","line":196,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SeparatedGauss.lean#L196-L243","doc":"**Gauss's lemma for disjoint variable groups.**\n\nIf `W` has integer coefficients and, over `ℚ`, factors as a product of a polynomial in the\n`x`-variables and one in the `y`-variables, then the two factors can be rescaled by a single\nrational `l` to become integral.\n\n⚠ The proof needs neither unique factorisation nor Bézout, and that is a consequence of the\ngroups being **disjoint**: `coeff (i ⊞ j) (U·V) = uᵢ·vⱼ` exactly, so once `U` is scaled to be\nprimitive and integral, the denominator of each `vⱼ` divides *every* coefficient of `U'`,\nhence divides their gcd, hence is `1`.  In shared variables the coefficients mix and none of\nthis is available. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.den_dvd_of_mul_int","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.den_dvd_of_mul_int","kind":"theorem","line":183,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SeparatedGauss.lean#L183-L194","doc":"If an integer multiple of a rational is an integer, the rational's denominator divides\nthat multiplier. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.exists_primitive_scaling","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.exists_primitive_scaling","kind":"theorem","line":143,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SeparatedGauss.lean#L143-L175","doc":"**A rational polynomial has a primitive integral scaling.** ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.exists_primitive_part","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.exists_primitive_part","kind":"theorem","line":107,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SeparatedGauss.lean#L107-L141","doc":"**The primitive part.**\n\n`p = content · q` with `q` primitive, where primitivity is stated as \"every common divisor of\nthe coefficients is a unit\" — which is what the Gauss argument below consumes, and avoids\nhaving to define `IsPrimitive` for `MvPolynomial` at all. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.intContent_ne_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.intContent_ne_zero","kind":"theorem","line":97,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SeparatedGauss.lean#L97-L105","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.intContent_dvd_coeff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.intContent_dvd_coeff","kind":"theorem","line":89,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SeparatedGauss.lean#L89-L95","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.intContent","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.intContent","kind":"def","line":84,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SeparatedGauss.lean#L84-L87","doc":"The **content** of an integer polynomial: the gcd of its coefficients.\n\nMathlib has this for `Polynomial` only. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:MvPolynomial.exists_int_scaling","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"MvPolynomial.exists_int_scaling","kind":"theorem","line":50,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SeparatedGauss.lean#L50-L80","doc":"**A rational polynomial has an integral multiple.**\n\n`IsLocalization.exist_integer_multiples` supplies a common denominator for the finitely many\ncoefficients; `Finsupp.mapRange` over `Rat.num` then builds the integral polynomial without\nany choice, because the numerator map is already correct at every coefficient — including the\nones outside the support, where it returns `0`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}